-- numbers.lth: the realizations of the numbers: what the core's operations on `Int`, `Float64`
-- and `String` are, the conversion from an integer, the elementary functions, and comparison
-- within a tolerance the demand sets.
--
-- Draft 0, 2026-10-14, by Claude Fable 5.1, an agent of the coordinating session, on the brief
-- record/IA/slice-theories-2026-10-14/prompt.md (item 1 of the inventory's §6). Sources: spec
-- 11.S1 (three objects under one name), 11.S2 (a leaf is declared over what it receives), 11.S4
-- (the operations of `Float64` are the machine's rounded ones: the refinement is of the values,
-- not of the operations), 11.S5 (`toFloat64`), 11.S7 (NaN and overflow are errors), 11.S13 (each
-- operation rounds the exact one, stated in `lib/rational.lut`, not written), 11.S15 (the doubles
-- are not a field), 11.S18 (a tolerance belongs to the demand, D-2026-10-12-h; the law returns
-- what it measured), 5.S7 (no law carries a cutoff), P11 (every criterion is a parameter);
-- 3.S17 and term.lth §5 (what the kernel computes); kernel_laws.lth (the pattern, and
-- `OrderedSemiring` for `Nat`); algebra.lth (the ring of `Int`, the field of `Real`). The v0.1
-- files are `real.lut` and `real_python.lut` at the tag v0.1-final.
--
-- What this file states. The core's operations have one model each, the interpreter's
-- (core.lut), so by D-2026-10-13-aa Q5 their laws could be plain definitions; they are gathered
-- in records all the same (`Doubles`, `IntegerDivision`, `Conversion`, `Elementary`), in the
-- pattern of kernel_laws.lth, so that a generic definition can take the operations of its number
-- type as one value (11.S3, `NumberModel`) and so that numbers_model.lut names the models. Of
-- the doubles only what is exact is stated with `==`: the operations commute, the identities
-- hold, rounding is monotone, the order is total; the rounding law itself needs `Rational`
-- (11.S13) and waits for rational.lut. Where only a tolerance can be stated (the elementary
-- functions, which the host's library computes to within a few units in the last place), the law
-- takes the tolerance as a parameter, `Tolerance`, bound by the demand (11.S18, D-2026-10-12-h,
-- as `Level` in generators.lth), never written as a constant (P11). `String`'s `+` is a monoid
-- (kernel_laws.lth), named in numbers_model.lut; `Int` is an ordered ring (algebra.lth), named
-- there too; here is what the ring does not say, the quotient and the remainder.
--
-- Bite 8 revision, Claude Opus 5.5, 2026-10-21, prompt-bite-8.md at 1429de8: §2 states the exact laws
-- by the core's properties and adds the within-bound laws of execution.md section 4 with their
-- ranges (D-2026-10-17-f, D-2026-10-18-j); `within` and `withinOf` are balls of `absDistance`.
--
-- Index (all sections written in this draft):
--   §1 Magnitude and tolerance: `abs`, `Tolerance`, `within`; the doubles of moderate size,
--      where every operation of two is finite (11.S7, the range of 11.S13).              done
--   §2 The doubles: the four operations and the comparisons, their exact laws (11.S4)
--      and those within a bound (D-2026-10-17-f).                                      done
--   §3 Integer division: the quotient and the remainder of the host (11.S4).            done
--   §4 The conversion from an integer (11.S5).                                           done
--   §5 The elementary functions `sqrt`, `exp`, `log`, `erf`: exact cases, bounds,
--      the identities within a tolerance (11.S2, 11.S18).                                done

import "laws.lth" Law Within

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------- §1 Magnitude and tolerance

-- the magnitude: `x` or `-x`, whichever is not negative; exact, negation being exact
abs (x : Float64) : Float64 := if x < 0.0 then 0.0 - x else x
-- the distance of the real line as the doubles compute it, `|a - b|`: the subtraction rounds, so
-- it is a metric only within a bound (`MetricWithin`, numbers_model.lut `AbsDistanceIsAMetricWithin`,
-- D-2026-10-17-f)
absDistance (a b : Float64) : Float64 := abs (a - b)

-- a tolerance is a parameter of the demand (11.S18, D-2026-10-12-h, P11): a law that needs one
-- takes a `Tolerance`, and the test binds it (7.R1); no law here holds a number of its own
Tolerance : U := { tol : Float64 | 0.0 <= tol }
-- equal within a tolerance relative to the size of the values, `|a - b| <= tol (1 + |a| + |b|)`:
-- the ball of `absDistance` of that radius (D-2026-10-17-f); with tolerance zero it is equality
within (tol : Tolerance) (a b : Float64) : Bool := Ball Float64 absDistance (fn x y { tol * (1.0 + abs x + abs y) }) a b
-- the same, with the scale the law names: a sum of terms is compared relative to the sum of their
-- magnitudes (11.S14), not to its own value, since the terms can cancel
withinOf (tol : Tolerance) (scale a b : Float64) : Bool := Ball Float64 absDistance (fn x y { tol * scale }) a b

-- the doubles of moderate size, zero or of magnitude between 1e-150 and 1e150: a sum, a product
-- or a quotient of two is finite (11.S7 makes an overflow an error, so a law over all doubles
-- would raise on its own cases), and the relative rounding law holds (the range of 11.S13,
-- `NormalDouble` of rational.lut). The bounds are the range of the doubles, not a criterion.
Moderate : U := { x : Float64 | x == 0.0 or (1.0e-150 <= x and x <= 1.0e150) or (-1.0e150 <= x and x <= -1.0e-150) }
NonNegative : U := { x : Moderate | 0.0 <= x }
Positive : U := { x : Moderate | 0.0 < x }
NonZero : U := { x : Moderate | not (x == 0.0) }
-- the doubles whose products of three stay in the normal range, zero or of magnitude between
-- 1e-100 and 1e100: the range where the product's rounding law holds for two products in a row
-- (Higham 2002, section 2.2: the standard model assumes no underflow or overflow)
Tame : U := { x : Float64 | x == 0.0 or (1.0e-100 <= x and x <= 1.0e100) or (-1.0e100 <= x and x <= -1.0e-100) }

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------- §2 The doubles (11.S4)

-- The four operations are the machine's, each the IEEE 754 binary64 operation rounded to
-- nearest, and the comparisons are the order of the finite doubles; `epsilon` is the distance
-- from one to the next double, 2^-52, twice the unit roundoff `u` of spec 1 (1.S9). The infix
-- forms of core.lut are the model (numbers_model.lut `doubles`).
Doubles (F : U) : {
  plus minus times divide : F -> F -> F,
  less leq greater geq unequal : F -> F -> Bool,
  epsilon : F,
}

-- What is exact (11.S4), stated by the core's properties in `Doubles`' claim below: rounding is a
-- function of the exact result, so `plus` and `times` commute (over `Moderate`, where they are
-- finite); zero and one are neutral and zero absorbs, over every double; negation is involutive;
-- `leq` is a total order (core.lut's `FloatIsOrdered` claims it of `<=`). What has no core
-- property, or needs a range the core's property cannot state, is stated here.
DivideOne (d : Doubles Float64) (x : Moderate) : Bool := d.divide x 1.0 == x
MinusSelf (d : Doubles Float64) (x : Moderate) : Bool := d.minus x x == 0.0
DivideSelf (d : Doubles Float64) (x : Positive) : Bool := d.divide x x == 1.0
MinusIsPlusNegated (d : Doubles Float64) (x y : Moderate) : Bool := d.minus x y == d.plus x (d.minus 0.0 y)
-- [proposed, 2026-10-15, L9.14] the strict order differs from the order only at a tie, which two
-- independent draws almost never give, so `less := a <= b` passed the law over every drawn pair
-- (report-mutants.md); the law is stated over a pair that ties when `tie` holds, its second the
-- first: the same instances as over every pair, drawn with an atom at equality of one half
LessIsStrict (d : Doubles Float64) (x y : Moderate) (tie : Bool) : Bool :=
  { z := if tie then x else y
    d.less x z == (d.leq x z and not (x == z)) }
GreaterIsLessReversed (d : Doubles Float64) (x y : Moderate) : Bool := d.greater x y == d.less y x
GeqIsLeqReversed (d : Doubles Float64) (x y : Moderate) : Bool := d.geq x y == d.leq y x
UnequalIsNotEqual (d : Doubles Float64) (x y : Moderate) : Bool := d.unequal x y == not (x == y)
-- a sum keeps the order of its terms, and so does a product by a non-negative factor (rounding is
-- monotone, 11.S4, L13.3): `Monotone` of the operation with `z`, since `MonotoneIn` needs the
-- operation closed on its carrier and a sum of moderate doubles need not be moderate
PlusMonotone (d : Doubles Float64) (z : Moderate) (p : Related Moderate d.leq) : Bool :=
  Monotone Moderate Float64 d.leq d.leq (fn x { d.plus x z }) p
TimesMonotone (d : Doubles Float64) (z : NonNegative) (p : Related Moderate d.leq) : Bool :=
  Monotone Moderate Float64 d.leq d.leq (fn x { d.times x z }) p

-- `epsilon` is the ulp of one: adding it to one is seen and is undone exactly by the subtraction,
-- adding half of it is not seen (ties to even)
EpsilonIsTheUlpOfOne (d : Doubles Float64) : Bool :=
  d.minus (d.plus 1.0 d.epsilon) 1.0 == d.epsilon and d.greater (d.plus 1.0 d.epsilon) 1.0
  and d.plus 1.0 (d.divide d.epsilon 2.0) == 1.0

-- What holds within a bound (D-2026-10-17-f, D-2026-10-18-j). Each law relates the exact
-- property to the computed one by the bound error analysis proves, a function of the arguments
-- written here from the model's `epsilon`, never a tolerance of the demand (P11), and states its
-- range of validity as the type of its variables: outside it the law claims nothing. The unit
-- roundoff is `u = epsilon / 2` and Higham's `gamma2 = 2u / (1 - 2u)` (Higham, Accuracy and
-- Stability of Numerical Algorithms, 2002, section 3.1, Lemma 3.1). A sum of three is computed
-- with two roundings, so each order of association is within `gamma2 (|x| + |y| + |z|)` of the
-- exact sum, the two within twice that, and the difference itself is computed with a rounding:
-- `3 gamma2` bounds it with room (D-2026-10-17-f as corrected on 2026-10-17; `gamma2` alone is
-- refuted, numbers_controls.test.lut). Products: each order is within `gamma2 |x y z|` of the
-- exact product, likewise. Distributivity: `x (y + z)` and `x y + x z` each carry two roundings
-- of terms bounded by `|x| (|y| + |z|)`. The reciprocal: `1 / x` and the product by `x` round
-- once each, so `x (1 / x)` is within `2u` of one (in fact within `u`, the spacing below one).
gamma2 (epsilon : Float64) : Float64 := epsilon / (1.0 - epsilon)
-- range: sums of moderate doubles are finite, and a sum that falls below the normal range is exact
PlusAssociativeWithin (d : Doubles Float64) (x y z : Moderate) : Bool :=
  AssociativeWithin Float64 absDistance d.plus (fn a b c { 3.0 * gamma2 d.epsilon * (abs a + abs b + abs c) }) x y z
-- range: `Tame`, so that neither product of two nor of three leaves the normal range
TimesAssociativeWithin (d : Doubles Float64) (x y z : Tame) : Bool :=
  AssociativeWithin Float64 absDistance d.times (fn a b c { 3.0 * gamma2 d.epsilon * (abs a * abs b * abs c) }) x y z
-- range: moderate doubles; a product that underflows errs by at most 2^-1075, below the bound of
-- any moderate `x`, `y`, `z` that is not zero
TimesDistributiveWithin (d : Doubles Float64) (x y z : Moderate) : Bool :=
  DistributiveWithin Float64 absDistance d.times d.plus (fn a b c { 3.0 * gamma2 d.epsilon * (abs a * (abs b + abs c)) }) x y z
-- range: a moderate double that is not zero, whose reciprocal is a normal double
DivideInvertibleWithin (d : Doubles Float64) (x : NonZero) : Bool :=
  InvertibleWithin Float64 absDistance d.times 1.0 (fn a { d.divide 1.0 a }) (fn a { d.epsilon }) x

-- `Doubles`' claim (execution.md section 4): the exact properties by the core's names, then the
-- laws above; the doubles are not a semiring (11.S15), so `Semiring` is not claimed
Doubles (F : U) (d : Doubles Float64) :=
  Commutative Moderate Float64 (structural Float64) d.plus & Commutative Moderate Float64 (structural Float64) d.times
  & Neutral Float64 (structural Float64) d.plus 0.0 & Absorbing Float64 (structural Float64) d.times 0.0
  & Neutral Float64 (structural Float64) d.times 1.0 & Involutive Float64 (structural Float64) (fn x { d.minus 0.0 x })
  & Order Float64 (structural Float64) { leq := d.leq } & PlusMonotone d & TimesMonotone d
  & DivideOne d & MinusSelf d & DivideSelf d & MinusIsPlusNegated d & LessIsStrict d
  & GreaterIsLessReversed d & GeqIsLeqReversed d & UnequalIsNotEqual d & EpsilonIsTheUlpOfOne d
  & PlusAssociativeWithin d & TimesAssociativeWithin d & TimesDistributiveWithin d & DivideInvertibleWithin d

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------- §3 Integer division (11.S4)

-- `Int` is exact (11.S4) and an ordered ring (algebra.lth, numbers_model.lut `integers`). What
-- the ring does not say: `/` and `mod` are the quotient and the remainder of the division
-- algorithm, `q * b + r == a`, the remainder smaller than the divisor in magnitude and of its
-- sign. [proposed] Floor division, which is the host's (Python's `//` and `%`); truncation (C's)
-- is the other reading, and one commit changes the sign law.
IntegerDivision : { quotient remainder : Int -> Int -> Int }
NonZeroInt : U := { d : Int | not (d == 0) }
DivisionAlgorithm (m : IntegerDivision) (a : Int) (b : NonZeroInt) : Bool :=
  m.quotient a b * b + m.remainder a b == a
RemainderOfTheDivisorsSign (m : IntegerDivision) (a : Int) (b : NonZeroInt) : Bool :=
  if 0 < b then 0 <= m.remainder a b and m.remainder a b < b
  else b < m.remainder a b and m.remainder a b <= 0
-- a product divided by its factor is exact
QuotientOfProduct (m : IntegerDivision) (a : Int) (b : NonZeroInt) : Bool := m.quotient (a * b) b == a
IntegerDivision := DivisionAlgorithm & RemainderOfTheDivisorsSign & QuotientOfProduct

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------- §4 The conversion from an integer (11.S5)

-- `toFloat64` is the nearest double to an integer, exact below 2^53: it keeps zero, one, the
-- order, and adds exactly while the sum stays below 2^52
Conversion : { convert : Int -> Float64 }
Small : U := { i : Int | -4503599627370496 <= i and i <= 4503599627370496 }
ConvertZeroAndOne (c : Conversion) : Bool := c.convert 0 == 0.0 and c.convert 1 == 1.0
ConvertAddsExactly (c : Conversion) (i j : Small) : Bool := c.convert (i + j) == c.convert i + c.convert j
ConvertSucceeds (c : Conversion) (i : Small) : Bool := c.convert (i + 1) == c.convert i + 1.0
ConvertKeepsOrder (c : Conversion) (i j : Int) : Bool := not (i <= j) or c.convert i <= c.convert j
Conversion := ConvertZeroAndOne & ConvertAddsExactly & ConvertSucceeds & ConvertKeepsOrder

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------- §5 The elementary functions (11.S2, 11.S18)

-- Leaves of the environment, declared over the doubles they receive (11.S2); their model is the
-- host's mathematical library (numbers_model.lut `elementary`, routes to Python's `math`). The
-- square root is correctly rounded by IEEE 754, so its laws are exact; `exp`, `log` and `erf`
-- are computed to within a few units in the last place by the host's library and no exact law
-- of them beyond their special values and bounds holds. The domains keep the results finite
-- (11.S7).
-- [wave C, bite C1, 2026-10-25, Claude Opus 5.5; D-2026-10-18-j] The identities no longer take the
-- demand's tolerance: each is a `Within` law (laws.lth §5) whose bound follows from the accuracy
-- the model declares, `ulps`, the most units in the last place by which `exp`, `log` and `erf` miss
-- the exact value (a unit in the last place of `y` is at most `epsilon |y|` for a normal `y`), and
-- from the rounding of each operation the identity applies (`u = epsilon / 2`). A property of the
-- model, so a field of it and not a parameter a line could loosen (P11 keeps it a value, not a
-- constant of the law).
Elementary : { sqrt exp log erf : Float64 -> Float64, ulps : Float64 }
-- where `exp` is finite and `exp (x + y)` too
ExpDomain : U := { x : Float64 | -300.0 <= x and x <= 300.0 }

-- spec 11.S2: special values, exact
SqrtOfSquares (e : Elementary) : Bool := e.sqrt 0.0 == 0.0 and e.sqrt 1.0 == 1.0 and e.sqrt 4.0 == 2.0 and e.sqrt 0.25 == 0.5
ExpOfZero (e : Elementary) : Bool := e.exp 0.0 == 1.0
LogOfOne (e : Elementary) : Bool := e.log 1.0 == 0.0
ErfOfZero (e : Elementary) : Bool := e.erf 0.0 == 0.0
-- the square root is correctly rounded (IEEE 754 §5.4.1), hence monotone; and in radix 2, with
-- no overflow or underflow, the root of a rounded square is the magnitude [proposed: a theorem of
-- binary floating point, Muller et al., Handbook of Floating-Point Arithmetic, to cite if kept]
SqrtMonotone (e : Elementary) (p : Related NonNegative (fn a b { a <= b })) : Bool :=
  Monotone NonNegative Float64 (fn a b { a <= b }) (fn a b { a <= b }) e.sqrt p
SqrtOfSquare (e : Elementary) (x : Moderate) : Bool := e.sqrt (x * x) == abs x
-- `exp` is positive, `erf` is odd and bounded by one in magnitude
ExpPositive (e : Elementary) (x : ExpDomain) : Bool := 0.0 < e.exp x
ErfOdd (e : Elementary) (x : Moderate) : Bool := e.erf (0.0 - x) == 0.0 - e.erf x
ErfBounded (e : Elementary) (x : Moderate) : Bool := -1.0 <= e.erf x and e.erf x <= 1.0
-- The identities, each within the bound error analysis gives it (D-2026-10-18-j), `k` the model's
-- `ulps`, `y` the logarithm or the exponent involved. Each first-order bound is widened by
-- `elementaryRoom`: the second-order terms (`exp` of an argument off by `Delta` is off by a factor
-- `e^|Delta| <= 1 + 2 |Delta|` while `|Delta| <= 1`, and `|Delta| <= k epsilon |y|`) and the bound's
-- own roundings (at most eight operations, a factor `1 + u` each, within `1 + 4 epsilon`).
-- - the root of a square: `sqrt` rounds once (IEEE 754) and the product once, so `s s` is within
--   `(1 + u)^3 - 1 <= gamma 3` of `x` relative to `x`, and the difference of two doubles within a
--   factor two of each other is exact (Sterbenz);
-- - `exp (log x)`: the logarithm off by `k epsilon |log x|`, carried by `exp` as a relative error,
--   plus `exp`'s own `k epsilon`: within `k epsilon (1 + |log x|) x`;
-- - `log (exp x)`: `exp`'s relative `k epsilon` is an absolute `k epsilon` after the logarithm, plus
--   the logarithm's `k epsilon |x|`: within `k epsilon (1 + |x|)`;
-- - `exp (x + y)` against `exp x exp y`: the sum's rounding `u |x + y|` carried as a relative error
--   and `exp`'s `k epsilon` on one side, two of `exp`'s and the product's `u` on the other: within
--   `(u (1 + |x + y|) + 3 k epsilon) exp (x + y)`;
-- - `log (x y)` against `log x + log y`: the product's `u`, the logarithm's `k epsilon |log (x y)|`
--   on one side, two logarithms and the sum's `u |log x + log y|` on the other: within
--   `u + (2 k epsilon + u) (|log x| + |log y|)`.
elementaryRoom (e : Elementary) (d : Doubles Float64) (y : Float64) : Float64 :=
  1.0 + 2.0 * e.ulps * d.epsilon * (1.0 + abs y) + 4.0 * d.epsilon
SqrtSquares (e : Elementary) (d : Doubles Float64) (x : NonNegative) : Law :=
  { u := d.epsilon / 2.0
    Within Float64 absDistance (3.0 * u / (1.0 - 3.0 * u) * x * elementaryRoom e d 0.0) (e.sqrt x * e.sqrt x) x }
ExpOfLog (e : Elementary) (d : Doubles Float64) (x : Positive) : Law :=
  Within Float64 absDistance (e.ulps * d.epsilon * (1.0 + abs (e.log x)) * x * elementaryRoom e d (e.log x)) (e.exp (e.log x)) x
LogOfExp (e : Elementary) (d : Doubles Float64) (x : ExpDomain) : Law :=
  Within Float64 absDistance (e.ulps * d.epsilon * (1.0 + abs x) * elementaryRoom e d x) (e.log (e.exp x)) x
ExpAdds (e : Elementary) (d : Doubles Float64) (x y : ExpDomain) : Law :=
  { u := d.epsilon / 2.0
    Within Float64 absDistance ((u * (1.0 + abs (x + y)) + 3.0 * e.ulps * d.epsilon) * e.exp (x + y) * elementaryRoom e d (x + y))
      (e.exp (x + y)) (e.exp x * e.exp y) }
LogMultiplies (e : Elementary) (d : Doubles Float64) (x y : Positive) : Law :=
  { u := d.epsilon / 2.0
    Within Float64 absDistance ((u + (2.0 * e.ulps * d.epsilon + u) * (abs (e.log x) + abs (e.log y))) * elementaryRoom e d (e.log x + e.log y))
      (e.log (x * y)) (e.log x + e.log y) }

Elementary := SqrtOfSquares & ExpOfZero & LogOfOne & ErfOfZero & SqrtMonotone & SqrtOfSquare & ExpPositive
  & ErfOdd & ErfBounded & SqrtSquares & ExpOfLog & LogOfExp & ExpAdds & LogMultiplies
